1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,755
At the end of 2007, four out of every ten mobile phones sold on Earth were made by

2
00:00:05,755 --> 00:00:06,509
one company.

3
00:00:06,959 --> 00:00:09,465
That November, Forbes put it on the cover.

4
00:00:09,465 --> 00:00:10,778
One billion customers.

5
00:00:10,778 --> 00:00:12,986
Can anyone catch the cell phone king?

6
00:00:13,436 --> 00:00:18,429
Six years later, Nokia sold its entire phone business to Microsoft for five point

7
00:00:18,429 --> 00:00:19,601
four billion euros.

8
00:00:20,051 --> 00:00:22,133
Nokia didn't lose to a better phone.

9
00:00:22,133 --> 00:00:24,910
It lost to a different idea of what a phone was.

10
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:27,466
This is Built and Broken.

11
00:00:27,466 --> 00:00:28,477
Episode two.

12
00:00:28,477 --> 00:00:28,983
Nokia.

13
00:00:30,083 --> 00:00:32,793
The story starts in 1865, in Finland,

14
00:00:32,793 --> 00:00:38,801
with a mining engineer named Fredrik Idestam and a mill that ground wood into pulp

15
00:00:38,801 --> 00:00:39,533
for paper.

16
00:00:39,983 --> 00:00:45,274
He built a second mill near a small town on the Nokianvirta river.

17
00:00:45,274 --> 00:00:48,640
In 1871, the company took the town's name.

18
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:49,121
Nokia.

19
00:00:49,571 --> 00:00:53,121
For the next century, Nokia was not a technology company.

20
00:00:53,121 --> 00:00:57,855
In 1967 it merged with the Finnish Rubber Works and the Finnish Cable Works.

21
00:00:58,305 --> 00:01:02,424
The new Nokia made paper, rubber boots, car tires, power cables,

22
00:01:02,424 --> 00:01:04,740
and later televisions and computers.

23
00:01:04,740 --> 00:01:06,156
It was a conglomerate.

24
00:01:06,156 --> 00:01:07,443
A bit of everything.

25
00:01:07,893 --> 00:01:11,409
But inside the cable business was a small electronics division,

26
00:01:11,409 --> 00:01:13,698
and it started building radio telephones.

27
00:01:14,148 --> 00:01:18,205
In 1987 it released the Mobira Cityman, a brick-sized phone.

28
00:01:18,205 --> 00:01:23,818
Two years later, Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev was photographed in Helsinki using

29
00:01:23,818 --> 00:01:25,102
one to call Moscow.

30
00:01:25,552 --> 00:01:28,234
In Finland, people nicknamed the phone the Gorba.

31
00:01:29,334 --> 00:01:31,215
Then everything went wrong at once.

32
00:01:31,665 --> 00:01:37,517
In 1988, Nokia's chairman, Kari Kairamo, the man who had pushed it into electronics,

33
00:01:37,517 --> 00:01:38,631
died by suicide.

34
00:01:39,081 --> 00:01:44,924
In 1991, the Soviet Union collapsed, and Finland, which had traded heavily with it,

35
00:01:44,924 --> 00:01:46,825
fell into a deep recession.

36
00:01:47,275 --> 00:01:48,706
Nokia was losing money.

37
00:01:48,706 --> 00:01:50,821
One of its bank owners wanted out.

38
00:01:50,821 --> 00:01:54,927
And that year, the Swedish rival Ericsson held talks to buy Nokia.

39
00:01:55,377 --> 00:01:56,452
The deal fell apart.

40
00:01:56,452 --> 00:01:58,816
Ericsson wanted the phones and the networks,

41
00:01:58,816 --> 00:02:02,256
but not the loss-making television business that came with them.

42
00:02:02,706 --> 00:02:06,573
It may be the most important deal that never happened in the history of mobile

43
00:02:06,573 --> 00:02:06,920
phones.

44
00:02:07,370 --> 00:02:10,646
In 1992, a new chief executive took over.

45
00:02:10,646 --> 00:02:11,685
Jorma Ollila.

46
00:02:11,685 --> 00:02:14,162
His plan was simple and brutal.

47
00:02:14,162 --> 00:02:17,118
Sell everything that is not telecoms.

48
00:02:17,568 --> 00:02:20,072
Paper, rubber, cables, televisions.

49
00:02:20,072 --> 00:02:21,145
All of it went.

50
00:02:21,145 --> 00:02:23,792
Nokia bet the company on one product.

51
00:02:24,892 --> 00:02:26,533
The timing was perfect.

52
00:02:26,533 --> 00:02:28,388
On the first of July 1991,

53
00:02:28,388 --> 00:02:33,954
the world's first call on a GSM network was made in Finland, on a Nokia phone.

54
00:02:34,404 --> 00:02:38,483
GSM was the new digital standard for Europe, and soon most of the world.

55
00:02:38,483 --> 00:02:40,749
Nokia knew it better than almost anyone.

56
00:02:41,199 --> 00:02:47,184
In 1994 came the Nokia 2110, and with it a ringtone you can probably hear right now.

57
00:02:47,184 --> 00:02:48,252
The Nokia tune.

58
00:02:48,702 --> 00:02:52,055
Nokia's phones were cheap to make, tough, easy to use,

59
00:02:52,055 --> 00:02:54,477
and they lasted for days on one charge.

60
00:02:54,477 --> 00:02:59,320
In 1998, Nokia passed Motorola to become the biggest phone maker in the world.

61
00:02:59,770 --> 00:03:02,164
In 2000, at the peak of the dot-com boom,

62
00:03:02,164 --> 00:03:05,667
Nokia was worth about three hundred and three billion euros,

63
00:03:05,667 --> 00:03:07,769
the most valuable company in Europe.

64
00:03:08,219 --> 00:03:11,628
For a country of five million people, it was enormous.

65
00:03:11,628 --> 00:03:16,741
At its peak, Nokia was about four percent of Finland's economy and around a fifth

66
00:03:16,741 --> 00:03:17,688
of its exports.

67
00:03:18,138 --> 00:03:21,079
Then came the phones people still remember.

68
00:03:21,079 --> 00:03:22,242
The 3310 in 2000.

69
00:03:22,242 --> 00:03:24,089
And the Nokia 1100 in 2003,

70
00:03:24,089 --> 00:03:27,988
which sold more than two hundred and fifty million units,

71
00:03:27,988 --> 00:03:30,793
one of the best-selling phones ever made.

72
00:03:31,243 --> 00:03:35,556
By the end of 2007, Nokia sold forty percent of all phones on the planet.

73
00:03:35,556 --> 00:03:38,923
One hundred and thirty three million in a single quarter.

74
00:03:40,023 --> 00:03:41,849
Here is the part people get wrong.

75
00:03:41,849 --> 00:03:43,675
Nokia did not miss the smartphone.

76
00:03:44,125 --> 00:03:45,936
It practically invented it.

77
00:03:45,936 --> 00:03:49,156
In 1996 it launched the Nokia 9000 Communicator,

78
00:03:49,156 --> 00:03:53,315
a phone that opened up into a keyboard with email and the web.

79
00:03:53,765 --> 00:03:57,199
Around 2004, according to a former Nokia engineer,

80
00:03:57,199 --> 00:04:02,007
a team built a prototype with a large touchscreen and internet access.

81
00:04:02,007 --> 00:04:05,578
Management decided it was too risky, and shelved it.

82
00:04:06,028 --> 00:04:11,665
And in 2007, Nokia's Symbian software ran on roughly six in ten smartphones in the

83
00:04:11,665 --> 00:04:12,077
world.

84
00:04:12,527 --> 00:04:14,121
So what was the problem?

85
00:04:14,121 --> 00:04:18,040
Nokia was the best company in the world at building phones.

86
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:18,638
Hardware.

87
00:04:18,638 --> 00:04:19,302
Factories.

88
00:04:19,302 --> 00:04:20,232
Supply chains.

89
00:04:20,232 --> 00:04:23,553
Dozens of models for every price and every market.

90
00:04:24,003 --> 00:04:26,692
Software was something you put inside the phone.

91
00:04:26,692 --> 00:04:28,541
And Nokia kept score the old way.

92
00:04:28,541 --> 00:04:30,110
How many phones did we ship?

93
00:04:31,210 --> 00:04:34,181
In January 2007, Apple announced the iPhone.

94
00:04:34,181 --> 00:04:38,368
A year and a half later, the first Android phone went on sale.

95
00:04:38,818 --> 00:04:41,608
By Nokia's old scoreboard, nothing looked wrong.

96
00:04:41,608 --> 00:04:44,223
It was still selling more phones than anyone.

97
00:04:44,673 --> 00:04:49,156
But the new scoreboard was apps, touchscreens, and developers.

98
00:04:49,156 --> 00:04:51,759
And there, Symbian was falling fast.

99
00:04:51,759 --> 00:04:54,869
Forty seven percent of smartphones in 2009.

100
00:04:54,869 --> 00:04:56,387
Thirty eight in 2010.

101
00:04:56,387 --> 00:04:58,339
Under four percent by 2012.

102
00:04:58,789 --> 00:05:03,658
In September 2010, Nokia hired a new chief executive from Microsoft.

103
00:05:03,658 --> 00:05:04,589
Stephen Elop.

104
00:05:05,039 --> 00:05:08,362
In February 2011, an internal memo from Elop leaked.

105
00:05:08,362 --> 00:05:12,197
He told staff that Nokia was standing on a burning platform.

106
00:05:12,647 --> 00:05:17,104
Days later, Nokia announced it would abandon Symbian and switch to Microsoft's

107
00:05:17,104 --> 00:05:17,904
Windows Phone.

108
00:05:17,904 --> 00:05:20,304
Its shares fell fourteen percent that day.

109
00:05:20,754 --> 00:05:21,640
Here's the trap.

110
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:25,903
Nokia had announced the end of its old phones before the new ones were ready.

111
00:05:25,903 --> 00:05:28,173
Why would anyone buy a Symbian phone now?

112
00:05:28,623 --> 00:05:31,558
The Lumia phones that followed were well reviewed,

113
00:05:31,558 --> 00:05:35,666
but Windows Phone never caught up with the apps on iPhone and Android.

114
00:05:35,666 --> 00:05:37,368
By the third quarter of 2012,

115
00:05:37,368 --> 00:05:40,890
Nokia had three and a half percent of the smartphone market.

116
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:46,362
In September 2013, Microsoft agreed to buy Nokia's phone business for five point

117
00:05:46,362 --> 00:05:47,869
four four billion euros.

118
00:05:47,869 --> 00:05:51,133
About thirty two thousand people moved to Microsoft.

119
00:05:51,583 --> 00:05:52,579
It didn't work.

120
00:05:52,579 --> 00:05:57,623
In 2015, Microsoft wrote off seven point six billion dollars on the deal and

121
00:05:57,623 --> 00:06:02,003
announced seven thousand eight hundred job cuts, mostly in phones.

122
00:06:03,103 --> 00:06:05,855
But this is where Nokia's story is different from Kodak's.

123
00:06:06,305 --> 00:06:07,418
Nokia didn't die.

124
00:06:07,418 --> 00:06:09,841
It did what Jorma Ollila did in 1992.

125
00:06:09,841 --> 00:06:15,146
It cut down to the thing it was still best at, the networks that carry the calls,

126
00:06:15,146 --> 00:06:18,682
and in 2016 it bought the French rival Alcatel-Lucent.

127
00:06:19,132 --> 00:06:23,411
Today, Nokia builds the equipment behind mobile networks around the world.

128
00:06:23,411 --> 00:06:26,534
The name on the phones is licensed to another company.

129
00:06:27,634 --> 00:06:30,118
Kodak lost because it protected its profits.

130
00:06:30,118 --> 00:06:32,771
Nokia lost because it measured the wrong thing.

131
00:06:33,221 --> 00:06:36,992
It counted phones shipped, while the market started counting apps, developers,

132
00:06:36,992 --> 00:06:38,829
and what you could do with the screen.

133
00:06:39,279 --> 00:06:42,861
Being the best at the old game is not the same as winning the new one.

134
00:06:42,861 --> 00:06:46,749
And sometimes, being the best at the old game is exactly what stops you from

135
00:06:46,749 --> 00:06:47,772
playing the new one.

136
00:06:48,222 --> 00:06:50,039
This has been Built and Broken.

137
00:06:50,039 --> 00:06:54,962
If you want the next story of a giant that saw the future and still fell, subscribe.
